## INTRODUCTION
Hollow microsized/nanosized particles are of great significance because of their unique structure-dependent properties and promising applications in many areas.1−9 Typical syntheses of hollow particles involve the growth of desirable shells with the assistance of removable or sacrificial template particles. To realize desirable functionalities and maximize the structural advantages, hollow particles with high complexity, such as multiple layers of shells or multiple compositions, are highly desirable. However, most of the available hollow particles prepared via multistep templating processes exhibit relatively simple configurations, such as single-shelled spheres of one composition.
Templating methods have been considered as the most representative and straightforward approach toward hollow structures, which involve the controlled assembling of the designed materials on various removable templates, such as monodisperse silica/polymer latex spheres and emulsion micelles.1,6,10 Although conceptually versatile, the templating strategy practically has quite a few difficulties, ranging from the tedious procedure to the incompatibility issue between the desired materials and the template surfaces. Meanwhile, several novel approaches toward hollow structures have been developed on the basis of different principles, including the Kirkendall effect,4,5 galvanic replacement,2,11 chemical etching ,12,13 ionic exchang e,3,14,15 self-assembly,16 thermal decomposition, etc.17,18 For example, template-engaged methods (i.e., galvanic replacement, chemical etching) create hollow cavities in preformed solid template particles via manipulation of the different physicochemical or chemical properties between the sacrificial templates and the shell materials.2,11−13 Such template-engaged approaches are very effective to form hollow particles with various shapes, yet their versatility would be largely determined by the variety of the available sacrificial templates and the precursors of shell materials.19
Notwithstanding the above-mentioned advances in synthetic methods, hollow particles reported so far with high quality usually possess simple configurations. Increasing the complexity of hollow structures, in terms of chemical composition and shell architecture, is highly desirable for both fundamental studies and realization of various functionalities. This objective could possibly be achieved via complicated procedures, such as repeated templating methods.13,14,20,21 Nevertheless, it is extremely challenging to highly integrate multiple compositions into the hollow particles based on the protocols established so far, since different materials with distinct physical/chemical properties could hardly be incorporated simultaneously into the synthesis process.
Herein, we demonstrate a general approach toward the largescale and facile synthesis of complex hollow cubic microsized particles (in short, hollow microboxes) via precise manipulation of the template-engaged reactions between a unique metal− organic frameworks (MOFs) template and different substances.
By controlling the reaction/diffusion kinetics, we have synthesized iron oxide/hydroxide hollow microboxes with hierarchical multishelled structures. Furthermore, the oxides/ hydroxides of several metals/metalloids (Sn, Si, Ge, Al, B, etc.) can be incorporated into the iron oxide/hydroxide microboxes by coupling the condensation reactions. Our results show the feasibility and versatility of template-engaged syntheses of complex hollow particles and would shed some light on the fabrication of other complex hollow structures.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals. Polyvineypirrolydone (K30), ${\mathrm{K}}_{4}{\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}{\cdot}3{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ HCl, $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ , $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SnO}_{3}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ ${\mathrm{NaAlO}}_{2},$ $\mathrm{NaBO}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{SiO}}_{3}$ was purchased from Kanto Chemical Co., Inc. The ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{GeO}}_{3}$ powders were prepared by a solid state reaction route in which a stoichiometric mixture of ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf C O}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{GeO}}_{2}$ was heated at $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ .22
Synthesis of Prussian Blue Microcubes. Prussian blue microcubes were prepared according to our previous report.23 In a typical procedure, polyvineypirrolydone (PVP, K30, M $\begin{array}{r}{\mathcal{N}\approx40000}\end{array}$ , $_{3.8\mathrm{~g}}$ ) and $\mathrm{K}_{4}\mathrm{Fe}\left(\bar{\mathrm{CN}}\right)_{6}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\bar{\mathrm{O}}$ $\left(0.11\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ were added to a HCl solution $(0.1\ \mathrm{M},\$ $50~\mathrm{mL}$ ) under magnetic stirring. After the mixture was stirred for 30 min, a clear solution was obtained. The bottle was then placed into an electric oven and heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The obtained blue product was filtered and washed several times with deionsized water and absolute ethanol and finally dispersed into $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol for further use.
Synthesis of $F e(O H)_{3}$ Microboxes at Room Temperature. The $\mathrm{Fe(OH)}_{3}$ microboxes were obtained by the reaction of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{[Fe(CN)}_{6}]_{3}$ microcubes with ${\mathrm{NaOH}}$ at room temperature. Typically, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of above $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{[Fe(CN)}_{6}]_{3}$ cubes suspension in ethanol was mixed with 17 mL of $0.02\mathrm{~M~NaOH}$ aqueous solution. After the mixture was shaken by hand for about $\textsf{S m i n}$ , the as-prepared product was collected by several rinse−centrifugation cycles. For synthesis of yolk-shelled $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes, a $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ solution with a different concentration $\left(0.2{\bf M}\right)$ was used. Hollow ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ microboxes were made by annealing the corresponding hollow ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes precursor at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ with a slow heating rate of $0.5\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ .
Synthesis of Multishelled $F e(O H)_{3}$ Microboxes under Hydrothermal Conditions. The hierarchically multishelled $\mathrm{Fe(OH)}_{3}$ microboxes were obtained by the reaction of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{[Fe(CN)}_{6}]_{3}$ cubes with $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ under hydrothermal conditions. Typically, $5\ \mathrm{\mL}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{[Fe(CN)}_{6}\]_{3}$ cubes dispersed in ethanol was added to $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of 0.2 M NaOH aqueous solution. The suspension was transferred to a 60 mL Teflon-lined stainless steel autoclave, which was then heated in an air flow electric oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{10\ \mathrm{h}.}$ After the autoclave cooled naturally, the as-prepared products were collected by several rinse− centrifugation cycles. For synthesis of multishelled ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes, NaOH solutions with different concentrations (2.0, 3.0, and 4.0 M) were used. Hollow $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes were made by annealing the corresponding hollow $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes precursor at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air for $6\mathrm{~h~}$ with a slow heating rate of $0.5\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ i n i n^{-1}}$ .
Synthesis of $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}/\mathsf{S i O}_{2},$ $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}/\mathsf{G e O}_{2},$ $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}/\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3},$ and $\mathsf{F e}_{2}\dot{\mathsf{O}}_{3}/\mathsf{B}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}$ Microboxes at Room Temperature. Compared with the synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes, $0.12{\mathrm{~g~}}$ of potassium stannate trihydrate was replaced by $_{0.12\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Na_{2}S i O_{3},N a_{2}G e O_{3},N a A l O_{2},}$ or $\mathrm{NaBO}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ respectively. The following procedure is the same as that for the synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes.
Characterization. X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were collected on a Bruker D8 Advanced X-ray diffractometer with Ni filtered $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ radiation $\begin{array}{r}{(\lambda=1.5406\mathrm{\AA})}\end{array}$ ) at a voltage of $40\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kV}}$ and a current of $40~\mathrm{{mA}}$ Field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM) images were acquired on a JEOL JSM-6700F microscope operated at $5~\mathrm{kV}$ . Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were taken on JEOL JEM-2010 and JEOL JEM-2100F microscopes. Energydispersive X-ray (EDX) analysis and elemental mapping were performed using the energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy attached to the JSM-6700F and JEM-2100F, respectively. Nitrogen sorption measurements were performed on an Autosorb 6B apparatus at liquid $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ temperature. The chemical compositions of multicompositional microboxes were measured using inductively coupled plasma (ICP) optical emission spectroscopy with a Perkin-Elmer ICP Optima 2000DV.
Electrochemical Measurements. The electrochemical tests were carried out in two-electrode Swagelok cells. The working electrodes consist of $70\%$ of active material, $20\%$ of conductive carbon black (Super-P-Li), and $10\%$ of polymer binder (polyvinylidene fluoride, PVDF). The electrolyte is $1.0\mathrm{~M~LiPF}_{6}$ in a mixture of ethylene carbonate and diethyl carbonate $_{1/1}$ by weight). A lithium disk was used as both the counter and reference electrode. Cell assembly was carried out in an Ar-filled glovebox with moisture and oxygen concentrations below $1.0~\mathrm{\ppm}$ . The charge−discharge tests were performed on a NEWARE battery tester.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Our strategy for synthesizing complex hollow microboxes is based on the template-engaged reactions between a unique MOFs template and different substances. The complexity of the resultant hollow microboxes can be achieved in two aspects, namely, the hierarchical multishelled structure and multicompositions with atomically integrated oxides/hydroxides of iron and several metal/metalloids, as depicted in Figure 1. Microsized Prussian blue (PB, $\mathrm{Fe}_{4}\mathrm{[Fe(CN)}_{6}\mathrm{]}_{3})$ crystals with a cubic shape (denoted as PB microcubes) are employed as the template. Route A illustrates the formation of hollow microboxes with different structural complexity, from single shell to multishell with hierarchical architectures. Route B describes the synthesis of multicompositional hollow microboxes consisting of uniformly dispersed oxides/hydroxides of iron and another designed element. These complex hollow structures are prepared by precisely controlling the chemical reactions between the PB microcubes template and a proper alkaline precursor (a strong base or a conjugate base of a metal/ metalloid oxide based weak acid), which will be discussed in detail shortly.
Prussian blue is considered as the first synthetic coordination compound (or more commonly called MOFs), which is a mixed-valence iron(III) hexacyanoferrate(II) compound with a face-centered-cubic (FCC) structure (JCPDS card no. 73-0687; space group $F m3m$ , $a=b=c=10.13\mathrm{\AA},$ $\alpha=\beta=\gamma=90^{\circ}.$ ). PB microcubes of about $1\mu\mathrm m$ with high morphological uniformity and phase purity (Figure 2A,B, Figure S1 and Figure S2 in the Supporting Information) were synthesized and applied as the template in view of the unusual chemical properties and the scarcity of fabricating nanostructured materials using MOFs based templates.23 PB is insoluble in water with a very small solubility product constant $\left(K_{\mathrm{sp}}=3.3\times10^{-41}\right)$ . However, in alkaline solution, PB undergoes an ion exchange reaction as described below:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{\mathrm{12OH^{-}_{(a q)}+F e_{4}[F e(C N)_{6}]_{3(s)}}}\\ &{\qquad\rightarrow\mathrm{3Fe(CN)_{6}4-\Sigma_{(a q)}+4F e(O H)_{3(s)}}}\end{array}
$$
The $\mathrm{Fe(III)}$ ions in PB associate with hydroxide ions to form insoluble $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ $\big(K_{\mathrm{sp}}=2.6\times10^{-39}\big)$ , which is driven by the high concentration of hydroxide ions in an alkaline solution. To investigate the transformation of PB in an alkaline solution, we first carry out the reaction using PB microcubes and NaOH solution (Figure 1, route A). The products were examined by FESEM and TEM. After reacting with a dilute NaOH solution (0.02M) at room temperature, the PB microcubes were converted into microboxes with large interior cavity (Figure 2C−E). Remarkably, microboxes with higher structural complexity could be simply obtained via a similar process with increased reaction and diffusion kinetics. For instance, when NaOH solution with a higher concentration $(0.2\mathbf{M})$ was used, yolk-shelled microboxes with well-defined porous cores were obtained as the final product (Figure 2F, Figure S3 and Figure S4 in Supporting Information). The reaction could be further promoted by increasing the concentration of alkaline solution and/or reaction temperature, which results in even more complex structures. After reaction at an elevated temperature of $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a 0.2 M NaOH solution, the product was mostly composed of single-shelled microboxes, of which the shell was constructed by sheetlike subunits (Figure $2\mathrm{G-I}$ and Figure S5 in Supporting Information). When concentrated NaOH solution of $2.0{-}4.0\ \mathrm{M}$ was used for the synthesis, microboxes with multiple shells and similar hierarchical architectures were formed (Figure $2\mathrm{J-L}$ and Figure S6 in Supporting Information). It is postulated that the inward diffusion of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ and precipitation of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ are promoted with higher temperature and alkaline concentration. Hence, after the initial formation of the outer shell, an intershell of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ would be precipitated before the Fe species migrate outward and join the outer shell. Eventually the multishell structures are obtained.
The chemical process that leads to the formation of hollow structures can be rationalized as follows: the ion exchange reaction first takes place at the interface between the solid PB microcubes and liquid alkaline solution, leading to the formation of a thin layer of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ on the PB surface. As the reaction proceeds, $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ions continue to flow inward to supply the ion exchange reaction, while the precipitation of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ clusters on the preformed $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ layer and the outward diffusion of $\mathrm{Fe(CN)}_{6}^{4-}$ ions are anticipated (Figure S7 in Supporting Information). If the reaction proceeds slowly, the whole PB microcubes are gradually consumed accompanied by the growth of the $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ shell, which eventually evolve into well-defined $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes with large interior cavity. Meanwhile, the inward diffusion of $\mathrm{OH^{-}}$ ions and the ion exchange reaction can be significantly accelerated in concentrated alkaline solution at an elevated temperature. In such circumstance, a secondary layer of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ would precipitate on the inner core prior to its complete consumption, resulting in the formation of yolk-shelled or multishelled structures.
The as-prepared $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes exhibit an amorphous texture as determined by powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) analysis, which could be easily converted to $\alpha{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ via a lowtemperature annealing process (Figure S8 in Supporting Information). The morphology, hollow structure, and sheetlike subunits are all well preserved, indicating the good structural stability of the $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ and as-derived $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes (Figure 3). The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image in Figure 3F shows a crystalline character with a lattice spacing of $0.25~\mathrm{nm},$ which is close to the interplanar distance of the (010) and (100) planes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . Such interesting structures render the $\mathrm{Fe(OH)}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes with relatively high Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) surface areas of $175{-}255\ \mathrm{\bar{m}}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ and 92−174, respectively (Figures S9−S11 in Supporting Information).
The ion exchange reaction between PB and strong base (e.g., $\mathrm{\DeltaNaOH}$ and KOH) only results in products with single composition, namely, $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ or $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . We further replace the strong base with the conjugate base of a metal/metalloid oxide based weak acid, which is a weak base in nature due to the partial hydrolysis. Under optimized conditions, the hydrolysis of the conjugate base and the ion exchange of PB might take place simultaneously (Figure 1, route B). To demonstrate the feasibility of such an approach, we select ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{3}$ as an example, which can partially hydrolyze to form tin hydroxide (or oxide hydrate) and generate an alkaline environment:
$$
\mathrm{SnO_{3}^{2-}\Sigma_{(a q)}^{+}}+(1+x)\mathrm{H_{2}O}\rightarrow\mathrm{SnO_{2}{\cdot}x H_{2}O_{(s)}}+2\mathrm{OH\Sigma_{(aq)}^{-}}
$$
If this hydrolysis reaction (eq 2) could be coupled with the ion exchange reaction of PB (eq 1), the overall process would result in the simultaneous and stoichiometric condensation of iron and tin hydroxide/oxide based on the following reaction:
$$
\begin{array}{r l}&{6\mathrm{SnO}_{3}^{2-}_{\mathrm{(aq)}}+\mathrm{Fe}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]_{3(s)}+6(1+x)\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\\ &{\phantom{\frac{1}{1}}\to3\mathrm{Fe}\bigl(\mathrm{CN}\bigr)_{6}^{4-}_{\mathrm{(aq)}}+4\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3(s)}+6\mathrm{SnO}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{(s)}}\end{array}
$$
To verify our hypothesis, the product of the reaction between PB microcubes and $\mathrm{K}_{2}\mathrm{SnO}_{3}$ solution was characterized thoroughly. As shown in Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{A-C},$ , the product also consists of hollow microboxes with smooth surface, however, the shell of which is slightly thicker and displays a denser texture compared with that of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes. Certain porosity is still expected in the shell, as suggested by the moderate BET surface area of $44~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even without any hierarchical or multishelled structure (Figure S12 in Supporting Information). Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX, Figure S13 in Supporting Information) analysis of the asprepared microboxes evidently reveals the presence of $S\mathrm{n},$ resulting from the hydrolysis of ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{3}$ as expected. The Fe/ Sn atomic ratio determined by the ICP analysis is about $2/3_{\cdot}$ , which is in good agreement with the stoichiometry of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in eq 3. This further suggests the successful coupling of the ion exchange reaction (eq 1) and the hydrolysis reaction (eq 2). The amorphous composite microboxes (denoted as $\mathrm{\bar{Fe}(O H)_{3}/S n O_{2}{\cdot}x\bar{H_{2}O}}$ microboxes) were annealed at $500~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air to convert to the oxide form (denoted as $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes, Figure 4D and Figure S14 in Supporting Information). The XRD pattern of the annealed sample (Figure S15 in Supporting Information) can be assigned to a mixture of $\scriptstyle\alpha-\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ (JCPDS no. 33-0664) and rutile $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ (JCPDS no. 41-1445), which is similar to that of previously reported mixture of $\alpha{\mathrm{-Fe}}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ .25 The broad diffraction peaks also reveal the low crystallinity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes, possibly due to mutual inhibition of crystal growth of the highly dispersed ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ . To illustrate the spatial distribution of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ in the composite microboxes, elemental mapping is performed on a representative $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microbox (Figure 4E) under TEM observation. The elemental mapping results (Figure 4F−H) demonstrate the generally uniform distribution of Sn and Fe within the microbox, which corresponds to the $\mathbf{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ crystallites. After selectively dissolving $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ with a $2.0\mathrm{~M~}$ HCl aqueous solution, single-compositional $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes were obtained as confirmed by electron microscope images (Figure $_{4\mathrm{I-L}}$ and Figure S16 in Supporting Information) and XRD analysis (Figure S15 in Supporting Information). The $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes remain intact, whereas the removal of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ makes the shell more porous as evidenced by the improved transparency of the microboxes and some visible pores in TEM images (Figure $^{4\mathrm{K},\mathrm{L}}$ and Figure S16B in Supporting Information) and the significant increase of BET surface area from $43~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes (Figure S17 in Supporting Information) to $89~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ microboxes (Figure S18 in Supporting Information).
The above analysis unambiguously indicates the high integration and uniform dispersion of iron and tin hydroxides/oxides in the composite microboxes. This compositional uniformity is derived from the simultaneous formation of Feand $S_{\mathrm{n}}$ -based species during the unique template-engaged process (Figure 1, route B). The alkaline environment provided by the hydrolysis of ${\mathrm{K}}_{2}{\mathrm{SnO}}_{3}$ triggers the conversion from PB microcubes to ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes similar to that in route A. Meanwhile, the consumption of hydroxide ions promotes the in situ and stoichiometric precipitation of amorphous $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}{\cdot}x\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ during the formation of ${\mathrm{Fe}}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ shell. Therefore, driven by such unique coupled ion exchange/hydrolysis reactions, two different compositions can be hybridized in atomic scale within the shell of hollow particles.
Remarkably, this approach to synthesize iron-based multicompositional microboxes can be extended to incorporate several other metal/metalloid oxides when the proper alkaline precursor is used. For example, we have successfully prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{GeO}_{2}$ microboxes as demonstrated in Figure 5, using ${\bf N a}_{2}{\bf S i O}_{3}$ and ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{GeO}}_{3}$ as the alkaline precursors, respectively. EDX analysis of the as-prepared $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{GeO}_{2}$ microboxes confirms the coexistence of $\mathrm{Fe/Si}$ and Fe/Ge elements in the composite microboxes with estimated atomic ratios of about $1/1$ and $4/3$ , respectively (Figures S19 and S20 in Supporting Information). Other elements from the nearby IIIA group, such as Al and B, can also be easily hybridized into the multicompositional microboxes using $\mathrm{NaAlO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{NaBO}_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ as the alkaline precursors (Figure S21 in Supporting Information). The synthesis is usually very facile and rapid, and the reaction can be generally completed within $10\ \mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. Furthermore, the present synthesis can be simply scaled up to achieve the high-yield, gram-scale production of these multicompositional microboxes. The versatility of this approach toward multicompositional microboxes provides the possibility to design and tailor the compositions and the derived functionalities for desirable applications.
The complexity of the hollow microboxes in both structure and composition might endow the materials with exceptional properties and performance in particular applications. Both $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ are important functional materials that have been studied in many areas, for example, as promising anode materials for lithium ion batteries.26−28 In this work, we further demonstrate the remarkable lithium storage properties of these hollow microboxes, which are associated with their unique nanostructure and high complexity. $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ can reversibly store lithium mainly via the alloying process after the initial electrochemical reduction to metallic Sn nanoparticles, while iron oxide can reversibly react with lithium through the conversion reaction.28 Despite the distinct electrochemical processes involved, they can generally deliver very high specific capacity $(800-1000\ \mathrm{\mA}\ \mathrm{\h}^{-}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ far exceeding that of the conventional graphite anode (theoretically $37\overset{\smile}{2}\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . However, the exceptional lithium storage property is accompanied by some severe drawbacks, including the huge volume variation upon lithium insertion/extraction that leads to the crack and disintegration of the electrode film and the low electrochemical activity of large grains.29 Previous studies suggest that the electrochemical performance can be improved svita f1o9,r30m,3a1 oTnh opf he $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ tmriuccrtoubroexs  nobdt/aoirn nafrno cmormoputoeA exhibit the representative electrochemical behavior of iron oxide, with reasonable cycling performance (Figure 6). As shown in Figure 6A, from the discharge−charge voltage profiles of single-shelled $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes, a distinct voltage plateau can be clearly identified at about $0.75\mathrm{~V~}$ during the initial discharge process, which shifts to about $1.0\mathrm{~V~}$ and remains stable in the subsequent cycles. Meanwhile, a poorly defined plateau is observed in the charge process at about $1.8\mathrm{~V~}$ . The discharge−charge voltage profiles are in good agreement with those in previous reports, which correspond to the reversible reduction of ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ to $\mathrm{Fe}^{0}$ and the simultaneous formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm O$ based on the conversion reaction described as follows: $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}+6L i^{+}+6e^{-}\leftrightarrow2F e^{0}+3L i_{2}O}$ . Figure 6B shows that multiple-shelled ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ microboxes exhibit significantly improved cycling performance compared to single-shelled and double-shelled $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes. The initial discharge and charge capacities of multiple-shelled $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes are found to be 1473 and $917~\mathrm{\mA~h~\g^{-1}}$ , respectively. The irreversible capacity loss of $35\%$ is mainly attributed to irreversible processes such as the decomposition of electrolyte and the inevitable formation of solid-electrolyte interface (SEI) film, which are common to most anode materials. From the second cycle onward, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes exhibit a high Coulombic efficiency of ${\sim}95\%$ . At the end of the $30^{\mathrm{th}}$ charge− discharge cycle, a reversible capacity of $650\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}~}$ can still be retained for the multiple-shelled $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes, while comparable capacities of 355 and 519 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are obtained for single-shelled and double-shelled ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ microboxes, respectively. One could probably attribute this reasonably good performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ microboxes to the thin nanosheet subunits that facilitate the electrolyte penetration and $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions transport in the electrode and the hierarchical hollow structures that could efficiently buffer the stress caused by the volume variation during the charge−discharge process. Notably, microboxes with multiple shells show better capacity retention due to the maximization of the beneficial effects arising from the hollow and hierarchical structures, which could better withstand the repeated volume expansion/contraction and facilitate the lithium ions insertion with reduced diffusion length.
## CONCLUSIONS
We demonstrate a general approach toward the large-scale and facile synthesis of complex hollow microboxes via manipulating the template-engaged reactions between the PB template and different alkaline substances. The reaction between PB microcubes with NaOH solution leads to the formation of $\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ microboxes with controllable multishelled structure. Meanwhile, PB microcubes react with the conjugate bases of metal oxide based weak acids and generate multicompositional microboxes $\displaystyle(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SiO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{GeO}_{2},$ $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/$ $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{B}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3})$ , which consist of uniformly dispersed oxides/hydroxides of iron and another designed element. Such complex hollow structures and atomically integrated multiple compositions might bring usual physiochemical properties. As an example, we demonstrate that these complex hollow microboxes, especially the $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ composite microboxes, exhibit remarkable electrochemical performance as anode materials for lithium ion batteries. We further anticipate that these hollow microboxes with high structural and compositional complexity would find their promising applications in many areas.
